==Abstract==

This NEP defines a common Neo N3 message-signing format, separate from transaction signing.

==Motivation==

Different message formats make wallet signatures incompatible. A common format makes message signing interoperable across implementations.

==Specification==

The keywords MUST, MUST NOT, and MAY indicate requirements as defined in RFC 2119.

===Message encoding===

The caller supplies a string <code>message</code> and an optional boolean <code>isBase64Encoded</code>, defaulting to <code>false</code>:

* <code>false</code>: encode as UTF-8 without normalization, whitespace changes, or added bytes. Reject unpaired surrogates.
* <code>true</code>: decode canonical standard Base64 ([https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4648 RFC 4648], Section 4), including required padding and zero pad bits. Reject input whose re-encoding differs from the supplied string.

Invalid input types MUST be rejected. Empty messages and arbitrary Base64-decoded bytes are permitted. The encoding flag is not signed; equivalent bytes produce the same payload.

===Signing===

Construct the following, where <code>||</code> denotes byte concatenation:

<pre>
PREFIX = UTF8("Neo N3 Signed Message / NEP-21 / v1\n")
P = PREFIX || UInt64LE(byteLength(messageBytes)) || messageBytes
signature = ECDSA_P256_SignDigest(privateKey, SHA256(P))
</pre>

The fixed prefix is 36 bytes, ending in LF (<code>0x0a</code>). The length is an eight-byte unsigned little-endian byte count.

The trusted signer MUST construct <code>P</code>, even if the message already contains a prefix. Callers MUST NOT bypass framing or submit a digest instead. Apply SHA-256 exactly once: an ECDSA interface that hashes internally receives <code>P</code>, not <code>SHA256(P)</code>.

ECDSA uses secp256r1 (NIST P-256). Encode the signature as <code>r || s</code>: two 32-byte big-endian integers, retaining leading zeros, for a total of 64 bytes.

==Rationale==

The prefix and length separate message signatures from transactions: <code>P</code> starts with an invalid N3 transaction version and is at least 44 bytes, unlike the 36-byte transaction signing data.
